content.thinking:
This is a critical finding! The Qwen3 chat_template has special handling for `enable_thinking`:

```jinja2
{%- if add_generation_prompt %}
    {{- '<|im_start|>assistant\n' }}
    {%- if enable_thinking is defined and enable_thinking is false %}
        {{- '<think>\n\n</think>\n\n' }}
    {%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
```

When `enable_thinking=False`, the template outputs `<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\n` to disable thinking mode.


When `enable_thinking` is undefined or True, it only outputs `<|im_start|>assistant\n` and lets the model decide whether to think. But RAGEN is manually appending `<think>` after applying the template, which creates a mismatch with what the model expects for the assistant turn format.

Looking at Qwen3's chat template, the model is trained to output `<think>\nreasoning\n</think>\n\ncontent` after the assistant marker, but RAGEN's environment instructions tell it to use `<answer>...</answer>` tags instead. The model doesn't understand this `<answer>` format since it was trained with its own thinking structure where content comes directly after `</think>`.

This format mismatch explains the 95% invalid rate — Qwen3 outputs its natural format with plain text after the closing think tag, not wrapped in answer tags. I'm also noticing the model duplicated the closing think tag in the v2 test, which suggests it's confused about what format to follow.

I should check the vLLM stop token configuration to see if it's properly stopping generation at the expected boundaries, since there's no explicit stop token setup visible in the rollout config. Looking at the Qwen3-1.7B base model specifically, it seems the issue is that without explicit stop tokens configured, it just keeps generating until hitting max_tokens. The real problem is becoming clearer: the base model doesn't naturally produce the `<answer>` tag format that RAGEN expects after the closing `</think>` tag. Instead of following that structured format, it either continues with unformatted text or repeats instructions. Since the regex pattern is looking for `<think>...</think><answer>...</answer>`, it fails to extract anything when those tags aren't present, which explains why the parsing breaks down.
